        "math_academy_id": "q-23896",
        "problem": "Simplify ${sec}^{2}\u2061x({cot}^{2}\u2061x-{cos}^{2}\u2061x).$",
        "worked_solution": "First, we expand the parentheses using the distributive property. This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sec}^{2}\u2061x({cot}^{2}\u2061x-{cos}^{2}\u2061x) & ={sec}^{2}\u2061x\\cdot {cot}^{2}\u2061x-{sec}^{2}\u2061x\\cdot {cos}^{2}\u2061x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}\u2061x}\\cdot \\frac{{cos}^{2}\u2061x}{{sin}^{2}\u2061x}-\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}\u2061x}\\cdot {cos}^{2}\u2061x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}\u2061x}-1 \\\\  & ={csc}^{2}\u2061x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, using the identity ${csc}^{2}\u2061\\theta -1={cot}^{2}\u2061\\theta $, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sec}^{2}\u2061x({cot}^{2}\u2061x-{cos}^{2}\u2061x) & ={cot}^{2}\u2061x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "actual_result": "Correct",
